// `Debug.inspect` on a Context, and the fault a spent deadline produces.
//
// # Why a fixture and not a corpus file
//
// context_values_report.nomi states half of it: `tests/` contains no
// program that fails an assertion, so the corpus checks only the passing path.
// This file adds the other half: a program that faults. A fault is not an
// assertion failure; it ends the run, and no corpus program does that either.
//
// So this file is not in the corpus, is never run by `nomi test tests`,
// and its non-zero exit is part of the recorded golden.
//
// # The two behaviours this fixture pins
//
// (1) `Debug.inspect(ctx)` prints `<context>`, the same text a `values:` row
// shows. `pub host type Context` gets a name-only `impl Debug` from the front
// end whose body is the static string `"Context"`, so a lowering that
// dispatched to that impl would print `Context` instead. `Debug.inspect` in
// ordinary code is not an assertion operand, so no assertion row covers this.
//
// (2) A spent deadline reaching main-line code reports
// `deadline exceeded: …`, and never
// `concurrent: cancellation reached a frame with no task to unwind`, which is a
// diagnostic about a builder bug. `rt.EnterDeadline` is a third way a frame's
// ctx closes, on the main goroutine while the body runs, which is the case
// `raiseCanceled`'s "not reachable by construction" argument excluded.
//
// # An already-past deadline rather than a sleep that outlasts one
//
// `Instant.from_seconds(0)` is 1970, so the deadline is spent before the
// `timer.sleep` is entered and the fault is immediate. A future deadline plus
// a long sleep reaches the same two behaviours and makes the fixture's
// runtime a race against a wall clock; this way there is nothing to wait for.
// It also exercises `EnterDeadline`'s already-spent path, where the fault must
// still report the deadline rather than a bare `context.Canceled`.
import {
    std/duration.Duration
    std/instant.Instant
    std/io
    std/timer
}

// The context is read and replaced as an application field, which needs a
// boot to name the application type.
struct Run {
    context: Context
}

fn boot(): Run {
    Run{context: Context.root()}
}

type TraceId String

fn main() {
    // The rendering, at the top level and nested in a container, because the two
    // reach different builder arms — contextInspectCall and debugInspector's leaf
    // — and only the first was found by hand.
    io.print("bare:   ${Debug.inspect(Run.context)}")
    io.print("nested: ${Debug.inspect([Run.context, Run.context])}")

    // A value link is invisible to the deadline walk, so the rendering is
    // unchanged by one and the fault below is unaffected by it.
    traced = Context.with_value(Run.context, TraceId("t-1"))
    io.print("valued: ${Debug.inspect(traced)}")
    io.print("value:  ${Debug.inspect(Context.value(traced, TraceId))}")

    with Run.context = Context.with_deadline(Run.context, Instant.from_seconds(0))
    io.print("spent:  ${Debug.inspect(Context.deadline_remaining(Run.context))}")
    timer.sleep(Duration.seconds(300))
    io.print("UNREACHED: the spent deadline did not stop the sleep")
}
